VI. Indicators of Progress
Student self-correction: student notices their own shallow reasoning unprompted, during or between sessions — the primary indicator
Student excitement: voluntary adoption of reasoning habits; discussing ideas with family and friends; returning to sessions with questions they generated independently
Transfer: student applies the causal-mechanism criterion to a new domain or context without being asked
Resistance to shallow answers: student noticing themselves giving a label without an explanation
Tutor redundancy: student can generate the Socratic probe themselves — asks "but why?" of their own answer before the tutor does

VII. Discussion
A. How This Differs from Conventional Tutoring
Different diagnosis of the problem produces a different intervention
The tutor's role shifts from source of correct content to diagnostic instrument and reasoning partner
The student's role shifts from recipient to active generator of understanding
B. Replicability and Training Requirements
The hardest thing to train is not the technique but the disposition; a question bank without genuine curiosity is an interrogation
Prerequisite subject matter knowledge: the tutor must be able to follow the student's reasoning into adjacent areas of physics, chemistry, and biology; surface familiarity is insufficient
Training sequence: cultivate kindness as the root; practice recognizing the description/explanation boundary; develop the analogy-hint repertoire; learn to sit with the student's silence without filling it
The method depends on one deep thing — genuine care for what the student actually thinks — not a checklist of competencies; this has implications for how tutors are selected, not only trained
Charisma as a surface performance, disconnected from the underlying dispositions, produces something the student will sense as hollow. So the behaviors are trainable, but they work best when they're expressions of genuine kindness and curiosity rather than techniques deployed independently of them.
C. Limitations
The method is designed for 1-on-1 engagement; in a group setting it can be preserved by directing the Socratic exchange at one student at a time while others observe, with observation itself being a form of learning
Tutor must have genuine working knowledge across the relevant sciences; a specialist in one domain cannot fully implement the method
The method is not appropriate as a first exposure to content; it requires that the student has encountered the material before
Empirical measurement of outcomes is not attempted in this paper; that is the subject of Paper 4
D. Relation to the Paper Series
